13
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:30.000
in Japan, Australia, New Zealand, Canada, and so on. And yet, same time, our outcomes are significantly worse than most of those countries in terms of, for example, how long our babies are likely to live who are born today. So, even though we are spending so much money, we are not getting the results that we really deserve with that. And that is a consequence of, in my view, the fee for service system, or what I also call paying for action. We're paying for action,

14
00:07:30.000 --> 00:08:10.000
but we're not really paying for results. What are the things that you're paying for? How did the system come about in the first place, Vivian? I think everyone's intentions were good in the beginning. So at the start, I'd say really took off with the Medicare, so with the government run program of health care for seniors. The security program to provide hospital care, nursing home care, home nursing service, and outpatient treatment for those over 65. Medicare will become law on July 1, 1966. The Medicare program, the government basically paid hospitals for services that were rendered,

15
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:41.000
and eventually also paid physicians for services that were rendered. So, every time we saw a patient in the hospital or in a clinic, we would send the bill over, and then we get paid. And in the beginning, that was really a pretty small part of the national economy. It was what you might expect at the start. But if you set up an economic incentive like that, you naturally tend to want to increase it's sort of, I guess, human nature, at least it is in our society, to try to generate more revenue.

16
00:08:41.000 --> 00:09:23.000
And so, people began to focus on revenue generating behaviors or revenue generating services, which were, for example, operating rooms, cancer centers, basically high-end care in hospitals. And so that's why over the, let's say, late 70s, mostly the 80s and early 90s, every major urban area was littered with giant cranes, building huge medical facilities. We called it actually a supply-generated demand. So the more operating rooms you had, the more MRI machines you had, the more patients were referred to those systems,

17
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:58.000
and so you could just continue to generate more and more fees and more and more business and more and more profit, frankly. So let's look at some of the attributes of this fever service monster in a little more detail. One of the fundamental principles that you identify is that more care is always better, because more care means more services and more services means more remuneration. Well, in a fee-for-service system, that's really what you're rewarding.

18
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:41.000
You are rewarding the more services. You're rewarding people doing things to other people. And actually in many cases, you're rewarding that regardless of whether it actually improves a person's health. So as long as you do a lot of procedures, as long as you poke in prod patients and do more colonoscopies or operations or administrative expensive chemotherapy agents, the more you do to them, the more money you make. And so the assumptions are more, more care is better, more visits are better, more treatment is better. And ironically, it actually might suggest that less prevention is also better, because if you don't prevent problems from happening, you're going to have problems

19
00:10:41.000 --> 00:11:25.000
that arise, and more people are going to come in to healthcare facilities seeking services. I'm not necessarily saying that doctors want people to be sick, but the financial incentives of the system seem to be almost against prevention in favor of actually treating problems once they arise. The fever service system really does create all kinds of unintended consequences and incentives, including what you've just described, which is to favor procedures against prevention, favors specialty care at the expense of, say, primary care or generalists. This is one of the reasons why orthopedic surgeons

